1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,360
After IVF, at the first ultrasound,
when do you see the heartbeat?

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,560
Usually around six weeks of pregnancy.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:13,220
And if it’s too early,
that’s not necessarily a bad sign.

4
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:14,560
I’ll explain everything.

5
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:15,780
But first,

6
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:18,700
a scene you all know.

7
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:23,780
Don’t deny it. You’re lying
down, the gel is freezing —

8
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:29,040
always freezing — it’s Article
4 of the Hippocratic Oath:

9
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:33,080
the gel must be stored in Siberia.

10
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:38,520
The probe is in place, the screen lights
up with a grainy black-and-white image,

11
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:41,700
like an ’80s TV…

12
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:45,020
and then: the doctor goes quiet.

13
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:48,540
Three seconds. That last three centuries.

14
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:52,640
Want to know what’s going on
in my head at that exact moment?

15
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:55,540
Most of the time, nothing worrying.

16
00:00:55,580 --> 00:01:00,000
I’m simply fighting the
little trackball on the machine

17
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:05,720
to place a cursor to the millimeter
at the tip of a tiny embryo.

18
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:10,140
It’s about the only moment of the day

19
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:14,060
when a doctor willingly
keeps their mouth shut.

20
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:18,740
But before that big silence,
there’s THE question.

21
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:24,180
The one that’s been on your mind
since the blood test said “positive”:

22
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:29,040
when is the first ultrasound after IVF?

23
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:32,180
And what should we really see on it?

24
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:37,980
Short answer: first a small
bubble in the uterus, then,

25
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:43,600
usually around six weeks of pregnancy,
a small cardiac activity.

26
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:49,380
And if the scan happens a bit early,
with no visible cardiac activity?

27
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:53,160
That doesn’t necessarily
mean something is wrong.

28
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:56,280
Sometimes it’s simply too early.

29
00:01:56,460 --> 00:02:00,620
Sometimes we need a follow-up
scan before we can conclude.

30
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:04,920
I’m Dr. Senai Aksoy, fertility specialist.

31
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:10,100
A quick note. In France,
when people say “first ultrasound,”

32
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:13,920
they often mean the twelve-week scan.

33
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:16,760
The one with the nice profile photos.

34
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:21,220
I’m talking about the VIP preview:

35
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:24,760
the one at your IVF center.

36
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:28,320
The very first one, before the “first.”

37
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:29,520
Question one:

38
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:31,560
the mystery of the weeks.

39
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:36,680
You had a blastocyst — a five-day embryo —

40
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:39,420
transferred exactly sixteen days ago.

41
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:43,280
You count on your fingers: sixteen days is

42
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:45,560
barely more than two weeks.

43
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:50,320
And then the doctor calmly
announces: “Congratulations,

44
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:53,720
you’re five weeks pregnant!” Excuse me?

45
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:55,840
You count again, on your toes.

46
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:58,260
Who added two weeks?

47
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:00,400
Has math changed since school?

48
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:05,580
No, nobody cheated,
and math hasn’t changed.

49
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:09,160
Welcome to obstetrics: the only field in

50
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:12,480
the world where your contract starts

51
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:15,880
two weeks before you’ve met the employer!

52
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:19,100
You get two weeks of
seniority, straight away,

53
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:21,520
courtesy of the house.

54
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:26,420
We count in weeks of
amenorrhea — the famous “SA”

55
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:30,480
— from the first day of

56
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:35,440
your last period, which is about
two weeks before fertilization.

57
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:40,740
Even when it all started
in a dish in the lab,

58
00:03:40,780 --> 00:03:44,140
under the embryologist’s watchful
eye, we keep that calendar.

59
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:49,440
It’s the Greenwich Mean Time of
pregnancy: not necessarily yours,

60
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:53,300
but every doctor sets their watch by it.

61
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:59,000
With IVF, we know the exact date to
the day, but we convert it anyway.

62
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:02,540
On the day a five-day
blastocyst is transferred,

63
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:07,600
you’re already at two weeks and five days.

64
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:13,140
With a three-day embryo,
two weeks and three days.

65
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:18,940
Two calendar weeks,
plus your blastocyst’s five days:

66
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:22,800
that’s your almost-three-weeks mystery.

67
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:28,600
And in France, to make it trickier —

68
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:32,460
it’s never simple —
there’s also a second count,

69
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:37,380
“weeks of pregnancy” or “SG,”
counted from fertilization.

70
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:38,880
So, two weeks less.

71
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,500
Two different clocks for a single uterus.

72
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:46,920
To spare you the headache,
here I’ll stick to the last-period count.

73
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:52,800
Key point: always ask your doctor
how many weeks pregnant you are,

74
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:57,340
and keep the transfer date and

75
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,680
the embryo’s age safe.

76
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:03,840
Every ultrasound milestone
is counted in those weeks.

77
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:08,120
Question two:
what should we see, and when?

78
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:12,240
At this stage, the scan
is usually done vaginally.

79
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:15,740
The probe is right next to the uterus,

80
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:20,520
so it sees much earlier
than through the belly.

81
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:25,140
On screen, at first, it’s a
bit like looking at clouds:

82
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:27,740
everyone sees what they hope to see.

83
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:31,740
The doctor looks for three
very specific landmarks,

84
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:34,380
always in the same order.

85
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:37,080
One: the gestational sac.

86
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:41,420
A small, round, dark pocket in the uterus.

87
00:05:41,580 --> 00:05:46,740
Around five weeks,
sometimes just a little earlier.

88
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:49,720
Two: the yolk sac.

89
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:54,460
Inside, a little ring
with a magic-spell name.

90
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:57,340
Usually a little after five weeks.

91
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:03,040
When we see the sac with
its yolk sac in the uterus,

92
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:07,200
it’s official: we’ve confirmed at
least one pregnancy in the uterus.

93
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:11,740
But after IVF,
even though it’s extremely rare,

94
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:16,880
we also check there isn’t a
second one in the wrong place.

95
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:19,020
Three: the embryo.

96
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:25,020
A few millimeters,
and inside it a tiny flicker:

97
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:28,240
cardiac activity,
usually around six weeks.

98
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,480
This table shows averages.

99
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:34,620
The early studies were small,

100
00:06:34,660 --> 00:06:37,340
but recent data confirm this timeline.

101
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:42,040
Very early on, being a
few days off is absolutely

102
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:45,120
not enough to draw a final conclusion.

103
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:49,200
Meanwhile, the doctor
counts the sacs, one or two.

104
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:53,220
And looks carefully beside the uterus,

105
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:55,440
toward the ovaries and the tubes.

106
00:06:55,480 --> 00:07:01,640
Question three: what if we
can’t see cardiac activity yet?

107
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:04,320
Sometimes it’s simply too early.

108
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:07,000
Sometimes the pregnancy has stopped.

109
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:12,560
And a single early scan can’t
always tell the difference.

110
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:18,600
That’s why the medical
criteria are very strict.

111
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:23,740
Measurements to the millimeter:
the size of the sac and of the embryo.

112
00:07:23,780 --> 00:07:26,200
Not mere impressions.

113
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:30,140
The NICE guidelines are cautious.

114
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:34,800
When a very early scan is inconclusive,

115
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:38,800
a follow-up scan is often planned.

116
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:41,280
At least seven days later.

117
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:44,980
The decision depends in
particular on the size of

118
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:47,600
the sac and of the embryo.

119
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:53,340
And waiting for that scan has no
negative effect on the pregnancy.

120
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:59,460
Of course, if you have
worrying pain or bleeding,

121
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:02,520
you don’t calmly wait a week.

122
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:04,860
A large American study,

123
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:08,760
published online in October 2025,

124
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:11,900
brings new information.

125
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:18,060
It looked at IVF pregnancies with
a clearly visible sac in the uterus.

126
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:23,980
From six weeks and three days,
if there was no embryo,

127
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:27,580
or an embryo without cardiac activity,

128
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:31,600
the pregnancy stopped
in every case observed.

129
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:33,480
That’s an important result.

130
00:08:33,540 --> 00:08:38,520
But it’s a retrospective
study, at a single center,

131
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:44,580
and these thresholds are
not yet universal criteria.

132
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:48,200
If your team offers a follow-up scan,

133
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:51,280
it’s not to prolong the suspense.

134
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:55,300
It’s to avoid a diagnostic error

135
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:58,840
and give you a reliable answer.

136
00:08:58,980 --> 00:09:03,860
I know perfectly well that this week
of waiting feels like an eternity.

137
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:06,620
Key point: at the very beginning,

138
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:11,080
an incomplete image
isn’t enough to conclude.

139
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:14,600
The follow-up scan is
there to clear up the doubt.

140
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:18,220
And if cardiac activity is visible?

141
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:22,340
That’s excellent news,
a real milestone passed.

142
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:26,440
But let’s talk numbers, frankly.

143
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:30,380
What’s your guess:
out of ten IVF pregnancies

144
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:34,580
where the heart was seen
beating, how many continue?

145
00:09:34,860 --> 00:09:38,580
These are broad statistical benchmarks,

146
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:41,820
not your individual situation.

147
00:09:41,940 --> 00:09:45,160
An older American study,

148
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:48,780
at a single center,
followed more than two thousand

149
00:09:48,820 --> 00:09:53,660
IVF pregnancies with cardiac
activity seen at around seven weeks.

150
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:59,620
Under 35, more than 9
in 10 kept developing.

151
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:05,180
Between 35 and 39, a little under 9 in 10.

152
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:10,460
And from 40 on, a little under 8 in 10.

153
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:16,280
A Chinese study published in 2016
found a similar order of magnitude.

154
00:10:16,320 --> 00:10:21,840
Out of more than two thousand
five hundred IVF pregnancies where

155
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:24,760
the heart had been seen,

156
00:10:25,280 --> 00:10:29,100
about 9 in 10 got past
the first trimester.

157
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:34,280
There too, the risk rose with age.

158
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:40,860
Some figures are a reminder
that caution is still needed.

159
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:45,120
But they describe groups,
not your personal story.

160
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:49,860
Seeing that heart beat
is a major milestone.

161
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:53,340
Not a promise carved in stone,

162
00:10:53,480 --> 00:10:56,820
but an immense step forward.

163
00:10:57,060 --> 00:10:59,500
Now, safety, straight talk.

164
00:10:59,780 --> 00:11:04,820
A pregnancy can sometimes
implant outside the uterus,

165
00:11:04,860 --> 00:11:10,260
most often in a tube:
that’s an ectopic pregnancy.

166
00:11:10,300 --> 00:11:12,920
In large IVF statistics,

167
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:17,380
this concerns about 1
to 2 pregnancies in 100,

168
00:11:17,420 --> 00:11:23,580
and even fewer after a single
frozen blastocyst transfer.

169
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:29,160
The risk is higher if
your tubes are damaged or

170
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:33,960
have been operated on,
or if you’ve already had

171
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:39,660
an ectopic pregnancy.
The early scan is precisely there to

172
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:42,580
check where the pregnancy implanted.

173
00:11:42,620 --> 00:11:46,400
And much more rarely — it does exist —

174
00:11:46,460 --> 00:11:51,680
there can be two pregnancies
at once: one in the uterus,

175
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:53,620
another outside it.

176
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:55,840
That’s a heterotopic pregnancy.

177
00:11:55,880 --> 00:12:00,580
With IVF, it’s a few cases
per thousand, or fewer.

178
00:12:00,700 --> 00:12:05,320
That’s why the doctor also
examines the sides of the uterus.

179
00:12:05,420 --> 00:12:09,680
So even after a scan in the morning,

180
00:12:10,180 --> 00:12:14,700
some signs should never wait
for the next appointment.

181
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:19,220
Sharp pain in the lower belly,
especially on one side.

182
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:23,940
Unusual pain at the tip of the shoulder.

183
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:27,840
Feeling faint. Or heavy bleeding.

184
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:31,740
In those cases,
you don’t ask the internet.

185
00:12:31,780 --> 00:12:35,980
Go straight to the emergency room,

186
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:42,080
or call 15, 112, or your
country’s emergency number.

187
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:48,480
For light bleeding or brown
discharge, contact your center.

188
00:12:48,520 --> 00:12:51,920
For persistent pain, even moderate,

189
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:54,440
also call your team the same day.

190
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:58,280
And your supportive treatments,
progesterone included:

191
00:12:58,540 --> 00:13:04,560
continue them as prescribed,
until the exact date set by your center.

192
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:09,100
Even after seeing a wonderful image,

193
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:13,560
you never stop your treatment
on your own initiative.

194
00:13:13,780 --> 00:13:15,240
The data stops here.

195
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:20,800
My clinical opinion starts now:
we all want to know everything,

196
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:23,300
right away. Me first.

197
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:29,000
When I’m waiting for a parcel,
I refresh the tracking every four minutes:

198
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:31,080
“Parcel at the sorting center…

199
00:13:31,320 --> 00:13:33,640
driver on the way…”

200
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:39,060
Does refreshing the screen
make the driver arrive faster?

201
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:44,740
Absolutely not. An ultrasound at
around five weeks can sometimes

202
00:13:44,780 --> 00:13:47,720
help us locate the pregnancy.

203
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:52,120
But if the question is “Is
the embryo developing well?”,

204
00:13:52,460 --> 00:13:57,160
it risks mostly leaving us
with an incomplete answer.

205
00:13:57,260 --> 00:13:59,600
Unless there’s a specific medical reason,

206
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:03,080
I prefer one well-timed ultrasound

207
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:08,300
around six or seven weeks to three
appointments booked too early.

208
00:14:08,340 --> 00:14:11,980
So, to answer the question from the start:

209
00:14:12,540 --> 00:14:17,300
the heart is usually
seen around six weeks.

210
00:14:17,340 --> 00:14:23,080
That’s why the first ultrasound is often
scheduled between six and seven weeks,

211
00:14:23,120 --> 00:14:26,420
depending on the center.

212
00:14:26,620 --> 00:14:29,400
And if it’s too early to see everything,

213
00:14:29,980 --> 00:14:35,180
a follow-up at least a week
later gives a clearer picture.

214
00:14:35,220 --> 00:14:38,960
Next time your doctor goes into their

215
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:44,240
big silence in front of
the screen, breathe calmly.

216
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:48,620
Most of the time, they’re just chasing a

217
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:53,340
millimeter with their cursor.
And if the silence drags on,

218
00:14:53,700 --> 00:14:58,720
you’re perfectly entitled to ask:
“Doctor, what are you measuring?”

219
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:02,960
For your appointment,
three questions to write down.

220
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:09,000
At how many weeks of pregnancy will I
be exactly on the day of the ultrasound,

221
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:11,980
and what do we expect to see?

222
00:15:12,020 --> 00:15:18,000
If not everything is visible yet,
when do we do the follow-up,

223
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:20,620
and who gives me the results?

224
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:25,160
Until what exact date should
I continue my treatments,

225
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:28,200
and who decides when to stop?

226
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:32,440
These questions are
summarized right below,

227
00:15:32,520 --> 00:15:34,560
in the video description.

228
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:38,800
Don’t forget, this content is purely

229
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:45,000
informational and doesn’t replace
a personalized medical evaluation.

230
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:48,160
It’s your doctor — they’re the boss.

231
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:49,700
Alright, ciao!
